    "text": "Let's denote the integer by \\( x \\). We need to find the integer \\( x \\) such that \\( x^2 < 2x \\).\n\nFirst, we rearrange the inequality:\n\\[ x^2 - 2x < 0. \\]\n\nNext, we factor the left-hand side:\n\\[ x(x - 2) < 0. \\]\n\nTo solve this inequality, we need to determine the intervals where the product \\( x(x - 2) \\) is negative. The critical points are \\( x = 0 \\) and \\( x = 2 \\), which divide the number line into three intervals: \\( (-\\infty, 0) \\), \\( (0, 2) \\), and \\( (2, \\infty) \\).\n\nWe test a point in each interval to determine where the inequality holds:\n1. For \\( x \\in (-\\infty, 0) \\), choose \\( x = -1 \\):\n   \\[ (-1)(-1 - 2) = (-1)(-3) = 3 > 0. \\]\n   So, the inequality is not satisfied in this interval.\n\n2. For \\( x \\in (0, 2) \\), choose \\( x = 1 \\):\n   \\[ (1)(1 - 2) = (1)(-1) = -1 < 0. \\]\n   So, the inequality is satisfied in this interval.\n\n3. For \\( x \\in (2, \\infty) \\), choose \\( x = 3 \\):\n   \\[ (3)(3 - 2) = (3)(1) = 3 > 0. \\]\n   So, the inequality is not satisfied in this interval.\n\nTherefore, the inequality \\( x(x - 2) < 0 \\) is satisfied for \\( x \\in (0, 2) \\). Since \\( x \\) must be an integer, the only integer in this interval is \\( x = 1 \\).\n\nTo verify, we check the square of 1 and its double:\n\\[ 1^2 = 1 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 2 \\cdot 1 = 2. \\]\nSince \\( 1 < 2 \\), the integer \\( x = 1 \\) satisfies the original inequality.\n\nThus, the only integer whose square is less than its double is \\(\\boxed{1}\\).",
